# pokezero gen3 fidelity fix: implement the TRANSFORM move (Ditto / Mew).
#
# Upstream poke-engine 0.0.47 defines Choices::TRANSFORM in the MOVES table and
# nowhere else: no arm in any generation's effect dispatcher, no tests. Clicking
# Transform in a gen3 rollout therefore produced a state change of exactly
# nothing, which is why it dominated the engine-search world-construction
# fallback census. Ditto (gen3 randbats: level 100, movepool ["transform"],
# Limber) and Mew are the two carriers in the pool.
#
# SEMANTICS, read off the vendored Showdown's `Pokemon.transformInto`
# (sim/pokemon.ts) evaluated at gen == 3 and pinned against the real Node sim by
# scripts/gen3_switch_differential.py (`transform*` scenarios). Gen 3 has no
# `transform` override of its own; it inherits gen4's
# (`transform: { inherit: true, flags: { bypasssub, metronome, failencore } }`).
#
#   COPIED : species, the target's CURRENT types, the five non-HP stats, all
#            seven boosts, the target's ability (`if (this.battle.gen > 2)
#            this.setAbility(...)` — a Ditto that copies Flygon is `-immune` to
#            Earthquake), and the moveset at `Math.min(5, move.pp)` PP per slot.
#            No gen3 move has a base PP below 5, so that is exactly 5.
#   NOT COPIED : HP, max HP, status, item.
#   FAILS   : only when the TARGET is itself already transformed
#            (`pokemon.transformed && this.battle.gen >= 2`). It does NOT fail
#            against a Substitute in gen3 (that guard is `gen >= 5`, and the move
#            carries `bypasssub`), it is not blocked by Protect (no `protect`
#            flag), and the USER already being transformed is not a failure
#            either (also `gen >= 5`) — so a Ditto that copied a Mew can
#            Transform a second time without losing the record of its base form.
#   ENDS    : on switch-out. `Pokemon.clearVolatile()` restores `baseMoveSlots`,
#            clears `transformed` and finishes with
#            `setSpecies(this.baseSpecies)`.
#
# MECHANISM. Everything is expressed through the instruction/delta system so
# search backtracking stays exact:
#   * species -> the existing (gen3-unused) Instruction::FormeChange
#   * stats   -> ChangeAttack/Defense/SpecialAttack/SpecialDefense/Speed deltas
#   * types   -> ChangeType, plus the TYPECHANGE volatile so the switch-out arm
#                that already restores `types -> base_types` does the revert
#   * ability -> ChangeAbility (and `ability_on_switch_out` already reverts it)
#   * boosts  -> Boost deltas (and `reset_boosts` already zeroes them on switch)
#   * moves   -> NEW Instruction::ChangeMoveId (a delta on the `Choices` u16
#                discriminant that also re-resolves the slot's cached `Choice`),
#                plus the existing DecrementPP for the 5-PP write. Upstream had
#                no reversible move-slot instruction at all: `replace_move` is a
#                test-only helper that mutates with no instruction.
#   * revert  -> NEW `Pokemon::pre_transform: Option<Box<PreTransform>>` holding
#                the transformer's own base form (species, five stats, four
#                (move id, pp) pairs — only what is not already recoverable from
#                `base_types` / `base_ability` / the boost reset), taken and
#                dropped by NEW Instruction::ChangeTransformSnapshot. Both
#                directions of that instruction are exact because it is only ever
#                emitted where the Pokemon IS in its base form.
#   * NEW PokemonVolatileStatus::TRANSFORMED, appended at the END of the gen3
#     enum so no existing variant's bitset index shifts.
# `size_of::<Instruction>()` stays 6 with align 2 (the engine's own size test).
#
# APPROXIMATIONS, deliberate and scoped:
#   1. WEIGHT IS NOT COPIED. Showdown copies `weighthg`. The engine's only gen3
#      consumer of `weight_kg` is Low Kick's weight-based base power, and Low
#      Kick appears in ZERO of the 393 gen3 randbats sets (220 species), so the
#      divergence is
#      unreachable in the pool pokezero trains and evaluates on. Copying it was
#      not free: a reversible f32 field would need either a lossy delta or a
#      9-byte instruction payload, and the latter breaks the 6-byte Instruction
#      size invariant.
#   2. PP GRANULARITY follows upstream. The engine only emits DecrementPP for the
#      move being used when that slot is under 10 PP ("most of the time pp
#      decrement doesn't matter"), so Transform's own PP cost is invisible above
#      that threshold. Transform's write of 5 PP into each copied slot is exact
#      and unconditional; only the pre-existing cost-tracking threshold is
#      approximate, and it applies to every move equally.
#   3. MAX PP IS NOT MODELLED because the engine has no max-PP field at all.
#      Showdown gives a copied slot `maxpp = calculatePP(move, this.ppUps[i])`,
#      i.e. the TRANSFORMER's PP-Ups at that index; nothing downstream of the
#      engine reads max PP, so only the (exact) current PP is represented.
#   4. A state built from OUTSIDE the engine that carries the TRANSFORMED
#      volatile without a snapshot (e.g. an engine-world constructor expressing
#      an already-transformed Ditto) cannot be reverted, because its base form
#      was never observed. That case degrades to "drop the volatile, keep the
#      copied form" rather than panicking or half-reverting. The snapshot rides
#      Pokemon::serialize/deserialize as an OPTIONAL trailing field, so such a
#      constructor CAN fill it in; an untransformed Pokemon serializes
#      byte-for-byte as it did before this patch.
#
# Touches src/instruction.rs, src/state.rs, src/gen3/state.rs,
# src/gen3/choice_effects.rs, and the Python binding
# (poke-engine-py/src/lib.rs + its .pyi), which gains an optional
# `Pokemon.pre_transform` string carrying the PreTransform wire record. That
# field exists for callers that CONSTRUCT an already-transformed active rather
# than reaching it by clicking the move: without it the engine has no base form
# to restore and falls back to approximation 4 below. Pinned by
# rust/pokezero-search/tests/gen3_transform_fidelity.rs.
# Authored against the confusion-patched tree (the 9-patch base); applied after
# it. The switch-out arm sits between the CONFUSION and TYPECHANGE arms — all
# three key off distinct fields, and TRANSFORMED's bit index is the highest so
# its revert is emitted last within the retain loop.
#
# Interaction with poke-engine-gen3-residual-defer-on-faint.patch: a transformed
# Pokemon that faints mid-turn owes a replacement, so the revert lands on the ply
# that RESOLVES the replacement rather than the faint ply (same end state as
# Showdown, whose faint() clears the volatile table either way). Pinned by
# a_transformed_pokemon_that_faints_reverts_on_its_replacement.
